-- title:
--   Producing switching tree data from an anchor
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Let $(K,T)$ be normalized minimal data, $A$ an anchor point for the resolvent tails, and $\mathcal U$ an ultrafilter on $\mathbb N$ finer than the cofinite filter.
--
--   1. **Switching tree data of an anchor.** The tree, weights and evaluation extracted from the tower of candidate stages started at $A$. Labels are the global candidate ordinals and functionals come from the candidate records. Returns, path displacements, prediction limits and detector inequalities are all inherited from the stage construction. In particular every retained node satisfies
--   $$
--   \varphi_v\bigl(f_v(a)-\Lambda_v\bigr)\ge1-2^{-(\ell(v)+9)} .
--   $$
